{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"algebraic-illstarred-mollusk","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context / Problem\n\nThe inbound fetcher can only see one tracker project. `jqls_for(project)` builds exactly one pair of\nqueries — active and recent-done — from a single key\n(`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/fetcher.py:78-90`), and `_validate_project_key` refuses an empty\nor malformed key outright rather than searching unscoped (`fetcher.py:63-75`, bug `626d`). A store\nthat lists several projects in its mapping still ingests only one of them.\n\n## Approach\n\nFan out: call the existing `jqls_for` once per project in the store mapping and union the results\ninto the snapshot, rather than building a single `project IN (A,B)` query. Two concrete reasons.\nFirst, the per-query truncation ceiling (`_ACLI_CEILING = 1200` at `fetcher.py:91`, raising\n`SilentTruncationError`) stays scoped per project instead of becoming a shared budget that a single\nbusy project could exhaust on behalf of the others. Second, it reuses `jqls_for` and\n`_validate_project_key` unchanged, so the JQL-injection guard that bug `626d` added keeps applying to\nevery key with no new interpolation site.\n\nInbound-created tickets record their source project in `bridge_project` and get their `repos`\ndefaulted from that project's mapping entry. The existing local-id derivation already encodes the\nproject (`_jira_key_to_local_id`, `inbound_translate.py:120-124`, `DIG-123` → `jira-dig-123`), so ids\nstay unique across projects with no change; this story makes the association explicit on the ticket\nrather than only implicit in the id.\n\nThe CREATE event for an inbound ticket is written directly rather than through `create_core`\n(`apply_inbound_records.py:150-222`, which stamps `creation_channel` at `:221`), so the two new\nfields are stamped at that same site.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/fetcher.py`\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/apply_inbound_records.py`\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/inbound_translate.py`\n- tests under `tests/`\n\n## Testing\n\nTier per the shared standard's external-service row: a stateful verified fake fed production-shaped\nJira fixtures. The fake must record the queries issued, because \"one JQL pair per project\" is itself\nthe contract under test and is invisible to a result-only assertion.\n\n- Happy path: a mapping with two projects issues exactly two query pairs — one per key — and the\n  resulting snapshot contains issues from both.\n- Postcondition: each ticket created from the fetch records the project its issue came from, and its\n  `repos` equal that project's mapping entry.\n- Edge: a project listed in the mapping that returns zero issues contributes nothing and does not\n  fail the pass.\n- Failure path: one project exceeding the per-query ceiling raises `SilentTruncationError` naming\n  that project, and the ceiling is evaluated per project — a second project under the limit is not\n  penalised by the first. This is the contrast case that proves the budget is not shared.\n- Collateral invariant: a store whose mapping holds exactly one project issues the same query pair as\n  before this change, so single-project behaviour is untouched.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A mapping with two projects issues one JQL pair per project in a single pass; checked by a fake\n      transport recording the issued queries and asserting the exact pair count and contents.\n- [ ] Each inbound-created ticket records its source project and the repos from that project's\n      mapping entry; checked by asserting both fields on tickets created from a two-project fixture.\n- [ ] The truncation ceiling applies per project, not as a shared budget; checked by a test where one\n      project exceeds it and a second under-limit project still ingests successfully.\n- [ ] A single-project mapping issues an identical query pair to the pre-change behaviour; checked by\n      a regression test comparing the recorded queries.\n- [ ] A configured project returning zero issues does not fail the pass; checked by an empty-result\n      fixture.","id":"1734-7c6d-5a6a-48fd","parent_id":"0e68-41eb-5782-4336","priority":1,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Multi-project inbound fetch: per-project JQL fan-out and source-project recording"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786160096505602001,"uuid":"1a129572-4d1d-473d-bcfd-400939a8dafd"}